From a00e3dbf1e64d9498290222ee7e67af12fdf1713 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 19:37:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08961/11362] exp6259: the goal veto is not the lever;
 consolidated note on the induction wall

The pre-registered kill condition fired. 16 candidates across 4 games: 14 admitted
by the live veto, 0 sensitive, arm B empty on 4 of 4. Arm A's selected candidate
found no plan on any game -- all four hit the 20000-node cap, because the
termination condition is never true. Non-hollow plans: 0 for both arms. There was
no difference to measure.

Adding a sensitivity condition to the veto rejects everything and leaves nothing to
select. Combined with exp6256, 22 of 22 freshly induced goal predicates fail to
fire on a real win. The gate is not selecting badly from a good pool; the pool has
nothing good in it. So the defect is upstream in how the predicate is PRODUCED, and
min_goal_predicate_consistency cannot help in either direction -- now measured
rather than argued. The live threshold stays unchanged and there is no longer any
reason to change it.

Deliberately NOT attempted: a fifth lever. Search structure, sampling breadth, goal
gradient, model architecture and the win exemplar were all tested this session and
all came back null or negative. Gate tuning would be the fifth thing tried around
the same wall.

Adds docs/research-notes/arc-induction-wall-consolidated-2026-08-12.md, the durable
artifact tying exp6248-exp6259 together: what was tested, the one thing that worked
(exp6250's ensemble selector), the systemic goal-predicate defect, the two
corrections this batch had to make and why the reasoning error is more reusable
than the result, and what the pattern says about where the remaining headroom is.

